Z||000000||BlackARROW||07-06-2001||12:06 AM||||Just another topic on that problem.  I keep looking for it - and when I talk about that problem to mechanics, they all look puzzled.<P>Let's say I'm driving on 2nd gear at 3000 rpm, suddently I press the gaz pedal to the floor until 4250rpm, than shift into third and still trying to accelerate big time.  Big time until I reach 3250rpm, when that sound comes out of nowhere, and I loose a lot of power. It's a scream. Friends following me could hear it. Well, I've put a microphone and recorded it (yup - just a maniac - anyone wants the file?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">, and found out that the sounds is coming off the exhaust pipe.  Maybe that sounds is coming from the intake, but exits through the manifold.<P>Here's what I thought it could be:<P>First I thought of the Thermac (cold/hot air distribution) - but I get only cold air all the time (broken mechanism), so that's not it. No obstruation there.<P>My PCV rubber grommet from the rocker arm cover had a crack and was leaking oil very slightly after some weeks. I've found out that could cause a vacuum leak - so I changed it.  Still it does my "turbo" sound.<P>My air cleaner was kindda floating on the carburator(TBI unit) - meaning that it didn't have a gasket for a airtight chamber, so I've worked out one tonight with proper Silicon gasket stuff. I'll see tomorrow if it did change something. While playing around, I've been looking at the intake manifold and it's surrounding, and I've found something in my Haynes book: the EGR.<P>Well the EGR..  Why the EGR?  What's it's purpose?  How the hell is it working, and could it cause that shrieking sound? <P>I was just thinking of the explosion - remembering an experience we did in highschool where we would lite hydrogen in a test tube, and it would explode while making a sound in an instant.  Well it's kindda like that, but put that sound constant while you pass from 3000 to 4250 rpm. Somewhere, talking about the EGR - it says "the EGR is used to lower NOX (oxides of nitrogen) emission levels caused by high combustion temperatures."  Is the NOX being recombustioned. After all, it is not NOS - what good is that?<P>I've never played with the ECM codes. But I think it's the perfect time to start.  <P>I know, I keep putting too much details - maybe. Thanks for the concern.<P>BlackARROW<BR>'85 2m4 <BR>(197000 km - close to a celebration  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||Steve Normington||07-06-2001||12:20 AM||||The EGR valve lets some exhaust gasses into the intake manifold to cool the combustion by enrichening the mixture. It reduces the NOX by preventing them from being formed in the first place. The sound you describe from chemistry class was more of a popping noise when we did it. If the turbo scream is like a series of pops very close together, it could be afterfiring. This could be many causes; mixture, ignition timing, etc. You might take a look at the plugs, their color will often give you a clue as to the combustion temperatures. Also, if I remember correctly, 4200 is pretty high for the 2.5, you may be experiencing valve float which is letting exhaust gasses escape during the power stroke.||||reg||1
